<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

The `checkoff` question type records credit for an in-person conversation,
demonstration, or other activity that a member of the course staff must
approve.  It is useful at milestones in a lab where students should explain
their reasoning, show working code, or discuss results before continuing.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
<b>A configured queue is required.</b>  A <code>checkoff</code> question is
not a self-graded question and students cannot submit it themselves.  The
course's queue integration must already be set up, and
<code>queue_enable = True</code> must be in effect in
<code>preload.py</code>, for students to request and receive a checkoff.
</div>

For information about installing and operating the queue, see [catsoop/queue](https://catsoop.org/git/catsoop/queue) or
[ichuang/catsoop-pyqueue](https://github.com/ichuang/catsoop-pyqueue).
This page focuses on authoring the CAT-SOOP question.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>A Minimal Checkoff</catsoop-section>

A typical checkoff appears after the work that the student will discuss:

<pre><code class="language-nohighlight">
&lt;question checkoff&gt;
csq_name = "diffusion_checkoff"
csq_display_name = "Checkoff 1"
csq_npoints = 10
csq_description = """
Discuss your diffusion model with a staff member.  Be prepared to explain the
update rule, the boundary conditions, and one test that supports your result.
"""
&lt;/question&gt;
</code></pre>

The principal settings are:

* `csq_name`: a stable name, unique among the questions on the page;
* `csq_display_name`: the label shown on the page and in the queue;
* `csq_description`: what the student should demonstrate or discuss;
* `csq_npoints`: the number of points awarded, defaulting to `1`.

`csq_description` is processed as CAT-SOOP content, so it can contain Markdown,
links, mathematics, and other supported markup.  Make the description specific
enough that students and staff share the same expectations.

<catsoop-section>What Happens During a Checkoff</catsoop-section>

With the queue configured and enabled, the normal workflow is:

1. The student clicks **Ask for Checkoff** beside the question.  The queue may
   ask for a table number or other location.
2. A staff member claims the request and meets with the student.
3. After the student completes the conversation or demonstration, the staff
   member marks the checkoff complete in the queue.
4. The queue sends an authenticated submission to CAT-SOOP.  CAT-SOOP awards
   the question's points, records who gave the checkoff and when, and locks the
   completed question.

The `checkoff` qtype intentionally provides no ordinary student Submit, Save,
or View Answer operation.  Students cannot award themselves credit by posting
a form value directly: CAT-SOOP validates both the queue's API token and the
staff member named by the queue submission.

<catsoop-section>Queue and Permission Prerequisites</catsoop-section>

Installing `catsoop-pyqueue` and its CAT-SOOP course integration is a deployment
step.  Once that setup is complete, enable the integration in the course's
`preload.py`, or in the `preload.py` for only those pages that use it:

```python
# preload.py
queue_enable = True
queue_room = "lab"
```

`queue_room` identifies the queue used for the page; choose a value that
matches the queue server's configuration.  Setting `queue_enable = False`, or
omitting the installed queue integration, means the **Ask for Checkoff**
control will not be available and the checkoff cannot be completed by a
student.

The relevant course roles must also have appropriate permissions.  In
particular:

* staff members who may approve a checkoff need the `checkoff` permission;
* the account associated with the queue's CAT-SOOP API token needs the
  `impersonate` permission;
* staff who use the staff-facing queue normally also need `staff_queue`.

For example, these permissions might be part of a larger course permission
map:

```python
cs_permissions = {
    "Instructor": [
        "view_all", "submit_all", "impersonate", "checkoff", "staff_queue"
    ],
    "TA": [
        "view_all", "submit_all", "impersonate", "checkoff", "staff_queue"
    ],
    "Student": ["view", "submit"],
}
```

The queue documentation describes its service account, API token, server
configuration, and deployment in more detail.  See
[ichuang/catsoop-pyqueue](https://github.com/ichuang/catsoop-pyqueue).

<catsoop-section>More Than One Checkoff</catsoop-section>

A page can contain several independent checkpoints.  Give each one a unique
`csq_name` and a clear display name:

<pre><code class="language-nohighlight">
&lt;question checkoff&gt;
csq_name = "model_checkoff"
csq_display_name = "Checkoff 1: Model"
csq_npoints = 5
csq_description = """
Explain the state representation and update rule.  Show one hand-computed
step alongside the result from your program.
"""
&lt;/question&gt;

...

&lt;question checkoff&gt;
csq_name = "results_checkoff"
csq_display_name = "Checkoff 2: Results"
csq_npoints = 5
csq_description = """
Show your final results and explain one limitation of the model or experiment.
"""
&lt;/question&gt;
</code></pre>

Place each checkoff after the material it covers.  This lets students request
staff attention at meaningful milestones and makes the queue label useful to
staff handling several kinds of requests.

<catsoop-section>Authoring Checklist</catsoop-section>

Before releasing a page with a checkoff:

* confirm that the queue service and course integration are configured;
* confirm that `queue_enable = True` is in effect for the page;
* confirm that `queue_room` matches an available queue;
* verify the queue service account and staff-role permissions;
* use a unique `csq_name`;
* state exactly what the student should show or explain;
* test the request as a student and the completion action as a staff member.
